# Goal

Create a separate Markdown student handout for **each practical activity on Day 1**.

The activities should allow students to apply the concepts that have just been introduced in the slides.

The course philosophy is:

> short periods of teaching followed by practical investigation and experimentation.

A normal teaching segment will be approximately **15–20 minutes**, followed by an activity.

The activities should therefore be meaningful rather than trivial, but should generally be achievable within the teaching schedule.

---

# Teaching style

These are professional software developers learning SDET skills.

Avoid turning the exercises into mechanical tutorials where students merely copy commands.

Prefer activities that ask students to:

* investigate;
* make observations;
* form hypotheses;
* explore the application;
* design tests;
* run experiments;
* examine failures;
* debug;
* compare expected and actual behaviour;
* discuss what good testing would look like;
* improve weak tests;
* discover defects;
* provide evidence for conclusions.

The exercise should tell students **what they are trying to achieve**, without always telling them exactly how to achieve it.

Use guided discovery rather than a long sequence such as:

```text
click this
type this
run this
copy this
```

Commands and code snippets are appropriate where students need a starting point or where setup would otherwise consume unnecessary class time.

---

# Activity structure

Create one Markdown file per Day 1 activity.

Use a consistent structure similar to:

```markdown
# Activity 1 — Exploring the Application

## Objective

What the student should learn or demonstrate.

## Scenario

Brief context explaining why they are doing this task.

## Your Task

Describe the challenge.

## Things to Investigate

Questions, behaviours or areas worth exploring.

These should guide without giving away the answer.

## Evidence

Describe what students should be able to show when the activity is complete.

For example:

- test results;
- screenshots;
- observations;
- defect descriptions;
- API responses;
- code changes;
- hypotheses;
- notes explaining behaviour.

## Stretch Challenge

An optional extension for students who finish early.

## Hints

A small number of progressively useful hints.

Do not immediately reveal the solution.

## Discussion

Two or three questions that the trainer can use when bringing the class back together.
```

Adapt this structure where appropriate rather than forcing every activity into exactly the same template.

